      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my sociology thesis and I'm getting a bit bogged down in the results section. Could you explain the practical difference between a p-value and a confidence interval? Specifically, if I have a p-value of 0.04 and a 95% confidence interval that doesn't include zero for my coefficient, how should I describe the significance of these findings in a formal paper?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, in your results section, you can describe the significance of these findings as follows: The p-value of 0.04 indicates that there is a 4% probability that the observed relationship (or one more extreme) is due to chance, which is below the conventional threshold of 0.05, thus suggesting that your findings are statistically significant. Moreover, the fact that your 95% confidence interval for the coefficient does not include zero further substantiates the result, indicating with 95% certainty that the true coefficient is different from zero, reinforcing the strength of the finding. In summary, both the p-value and confidence interval support the significance of your results, conveying that the observed effect is unlikely due to random chance and is likely present in the population from which your sample was drawn.",
